WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.560
AWS CLI commands run against a selected
account, region, profile,

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.912
and credential context.

3
00:00:08.992 --> 00:00:15.160
In automation, those values may come from
AWS configuration files,

4
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:22.707
shared credentials files, environment
variables, SSO sessions, assumed roles,

5
00:00:22.787 --> 00:00:26.791
CI/CD variables, or active local profiles.

6
00:00:26.871 --> 00:00:34.013
A secure script should not assume that the
current default profile or region is

7
00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:37.880
correct just because a command would run.

8
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.804
Target validation should happen before any
meaningful change.

9
00:00:43.884 --> 00:00:50.701
The script should confirm the expected
account, partition when relevant,

10
00:00:50.781 --> 00:00:57.707
region, profile name, environment label,
role, and resource identifiers.

11
00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:05.253
A deployment or operations script should
make it hard to mix a production resource

12
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:12.259
name with a development profile, or a
staging role with a production action.

13
00:01:12.339 --> 00:01:19.805
The target should be visible before the
script reaches an AWS API call that changes

14
00:01:19.885 --> 00:01:20.535
state.

15
00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:27.432
Development, staging, and production
should have separate assumptions,

16
00:01:27.512 --> 00:01:31.732
credentials, variables, and approval
paths.

17
00:01:31.812 --> 00:01:37.115
A fast local workflow may be appropriate
for development,

18
00:01:37.195 --> 00:01:43.688
but a production workflow may need a
release ticket, protected branch,

19
00:01:43.768 --> 00:01:50.368
change window, operator confirmation, or
CI/CD environment approval.

20
00:01:50.448 --> 00:01:58.348
The script should reflect those
differences instead of relying on naming conventions

21
00:01:58.428 --> 00:01:59.077
alone.

22
00:01:59.157 --> 00:02:05.650
Environment variables and CI/CD variables
should be treated as input,

23
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.002
not unquestioned truth.

24
00:02:08.082 --> 00:02:15.657
They can be missing, stale, inherited, or
inconsistent with the requested action.

25
00:02:15.737 --> 00:02:22.771
A good AWS CLI helper validates target
context early and stops if the account,

26
00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:29.235
region, profile, role, or resource does
not match the approved scope.

27
00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:35.700
The safest wrong-target change is the one
that fails before it starts.
